From c4d3b6422b3fbf13cb905d8c77cd628db8fb0ed4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 22:19:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Damage only the cells that changed, and say the window is
 opaque

Every redraw told the compositor that the whole surface had changed, so
a single typed character made it take in the full frame again. On a
compositor that uploads the damage to a display over a slow bus, such as
a 240x320 panel on a PDA, that is about 150 KB and 17 ms for one letter.

Work out what changed from the cells themselves instead of from libtsm's
ages. libtsm stamps the whole screen on every erase, scroll, selection
and colour change (the "more sophisticated ageing" TODOs), so its ages
say "everything" exactly when text is edited at a prompt or selected.
Each cell is reduced to a key of what decides its pixels: the symbol, its
width and the two colours with the cursor, selection and inverse mode
already applied. Two cells with equal keys paint equal pixels.

Two things are kept per key, because they differ. Each buffer remembers
the keys it was painted with, and only cells whose key changed there are
painted again. The frame the compositor shows is the last one committed,
and only cells that differ from that are damaged. With two buffers the
buffer drawn into is often older than the screen, so a cell can need
painting without needing damage, and a cell can need damage without
needing painting when it changes back to what the buffer already had.

The damage is one rectangle per run of changed rows, merged into a
bounding box when there are more than eight, and the whole surface when
three quarters of the cells changed, as before. A frame in which nothing
changed is no longer committed. Everything that makes the compositor's
picture unknown (first frame, a new buffer size, a font or scale change,
a changed margin colour) damages and paints everything.

Buffers are now checked one by one for their size and for having memory
at all. The old counter that re-created two buffers on a resize did so
for the same buffer twice when the compositor released at once, leaving
the other one without memory or at the old size for when it was needed.

Cells that cover pixels together are handled together: a narrow
character written over half of a wide one no longer leaves the other
half on screen, and a wide cell in the last column is clipped instead of
being written into the next row of pixels.

When the opacity is 255 nothing is blended, so say so: set the opaque
region to the whole window and use XRGB8888 buffers, which lets the
compositor copy instead of blend. Other opacities are unchanged.

HAVOC_FULL_REDRAW in the environment paints and damages everything into a
cleared buffer on every redraw. It exists for tests, to have a picture
the incremental one must equal.

